[38;5;231m⏺[39m Bounded task — one function, one caller, the flow is already here. I'll present a short design in chat rather than
  write a spec. But there's a fork I need you to settle first.

  [1mThe code as it stands[0m ([38;5;153mapp.js:4[39m, one caller at [38;5;153mapp.js:23[39m):

  [34mfunction[39m [33mlogin[39m(username, password) { ... [34mreturn[39m { [36msuccess[39m: [34mtrue[39m, [36muser[39m: username }; }

  [1mThe fork:[0m [38;5;153mlogin[39m is the thing that [3mestablishes[0m who the user is. Its only caller is the submit handler, which has
  exactly two values — [38;5;153musername[39m and [38;5;153mpassword[39m from the form. There is no [38;5;153muserId[39m anywhere in the repo for a caller to pass
  in, so "add a userId parameter" needs a source that doesn't exist yet, and which source you pick changes the
  interface and the form.

  - [1mReturn the userId instead of accepting one[0m — [38;5;153mlogin[39m (eventually the API) is what knows the real user ID; it comes
    back in the response and you log it there. Buys you real identity tracking with no new form field and no signature
    change. Costs: doesn't literally add a parameter. Rules out nothing — cheap to change later.
  - [1mClient-minted correlation ID[0m — the caller generates a per-attempt ID ([38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m) and passes it in, so a
    login attempt is traceable in logs [3mbefore[0m identity is known, including failures. Buys you failed-attempt tracing.
    Costs: it's a request ID, not a user ID — naming it [38;5;153muserId[39m will mislead whoever reads it next. Cheap to change.
  - [1mMake userId the credential[0m — add a [38;5;153muserId[39m field to the form and the login signature, with [38;5;153musername[39m demoted to a
    display name. Buys you a literal [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter. Costs: new form input in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, changed signature, and it
    re-shapes what your auth identifier [3mis[0m. This is the expensive one to walk back.

  [1mMy recommendation: return the userId.[0m What you asked for — knowing who logged in — is information the login call
  produces, not information the caller has. Passing in an ID the caller invented gives you a value that tracks nothing
  real. If you also want failed attempts traceable, the correlation ID stacks on top of it cleanly later.

  Which way do you want to go?

